module: subscription-billing

purpose:
  Let customers subscribe to a plan, be charged each month, keep access for a grace period when a payment fails, cancel, and pay a fair amount when they change plan.

rules:
  - A subscription is on exactly one plan at a time.
    proved-by: plan-change-same-day
  - A subscription is charged the plan price once for each monthly billing period.
    proved-by: monthly-charge, no-double-charge
  - A failed payment starts a grace period of 7 days and access continues during it.
    proved-by: failed-payment-grace
  - A subscription whose grace period ends without payment is suspended.
    proved-by: grace-expired
  - A cancelled subscription is never charged again.
    proved-by: cancel-stops-charges
  - A cancelled subscription keeps access until the end of the paid period.
    proved-by: cancel-keeps-access

workflow: charge-monthly
  input:
    subscription
  steps:
    find subscriptions whose billing period has ended
    charge the plan price
    start the next billing period
  failure:
    The charge is recorded as failed and a grace period starts.
  recovery:
    The charge may be tried again during the grace period.
  after:
    A paid subscription has a new billing period.
    A failed subscription has a grace period.

workflow: cancel-subscription
  input:
    subscription
  steps:
    mark the subscription as cancelled
    stop future charges
  failure:
    The subscription stays active and nothing changes.
  recovery:
    The customer may ask to cancel again.
  after:
    The subscription is cancelled and has no future charges.

workflow: change-plan
  input:
    subscription
    new-plan
  steps:
    work out the proration amount
    move the subscription to the new plan
  failure:
    The subscription stays on the old plan and no amount is charged.
  recovery:
    The customer may ask to change plan again.
  after:
    The subscription is on the new plan.

example: monthly-charge
  given:
    An active subscription on a plan that costs 20 whose billing period has ended.
  when:
    The monthly charge runs.
  then:
    The customer is charged 20 and a new billing period starts.

example: no-double-charge
  given:
    A subscription already charged for the current billing period.
  when:
    The monthly charge runs again.
  then:
    The customer is not charged a second time.

example: failed-payment-grace
  given:
    An active subscription whose monthly payment fails.
  when:
    The monthly charge runs.
  then:
    A grace period of 7 days starts and the customer keeps access.

example: grace-expired
  given:
    A subscription whose grace period ended 1 day ago with no payment.
  when:
    The daily check runs.
  then:
    The subscription is suspended and access stops.

example: cancel-stops-charges
  given:
    A subscription that the customer has cancelled.
  when:
    The next billing period would start.
  then:
    The customer is not charged.

example: cancel-keeps-access
  given:
    A subscription cancelled 10 days before the end of the paid period.
  when:
    The customer uses the service on the next day.
  then:
    Access is allowed.

example: plan-change-same-day
  given:
    A subscription on the basic plan.
  when:
    The customer changes to the pro plan.
  then:
    The subscription is on the pro plan only.

uncertainty: proration-method
  blocking: yes
  question:
    When a customer upgrades in the middle of a period, is the unused part of the old plan credited by day or by month?

uncertainty: downgrade-timing
  blocking: yes
  question:
    Does a downgrade start now with a credit, or at the end of the billing period?

uncertainty: reactivation-after-suspend
  blocking: no
  question:
    May a suspended customer come back on the old plan price?
